    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "size",
        "attrs": {
         "pt": 8
        }
       }
      ],
      "text": "Paresis vs Paralysis: paresis = partial weakness (strength grades 1–4); paralysis = complete loss of voluntary mvt (grade 0)"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 36,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": {
      "rule": "auto",
      "value": 1
     },
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "-",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "size",
        "attrs": {
         "pt": 8
        }
       }
      ],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "size",
        "attrs": {
         "pt": 8
        }
       }
      ],
      "text": "Ataxia vs Dysmetria: ataxia = impaired coordination of mvt/gait (wide-based, veering); dysmetria = inability to judge distance, causing over/undershooting on finger-to-nose or heel-to-shin (ipsilat cerebellar lesion)"
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 36,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": {
      "rule": "auto",
      "value": 1
     },
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "-",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "size",
        "attrs": {
         "pt": 8
        }
       }
      ],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "size",
        "attrs": {
         "pt": 8
        }
       }
      ],
      "text": "Aphasia vs Dysarthria: aphasia = language disorder (impaired production and/or comprehension) from cortical lesion (Broca’s or Wernicke’s); dysarthria = motor-speech disorder – slurred speech from muscle weakness/incoordination – language intact"
     }
    ]
   },
